  "slug": "browser-cheminformatics-aaf5",
  "summary": "Single-cell transcriptomics, improved genomes, and connectomes now each constrain inference to a specific biological unit of analysis.",
  "synthesis": "The three items share a concern with the resolution at which biological inference is drawn rather than a common software pipeline. The C. elegans preprint restricts scRNA-seq analysis to high-coverage cells with more than 15000 UMIs to quantify cell-specific mRNA landscapes across five datasets. The Stentor coeruleus study pairs an improved contiguous macronuclear genome with time-resolved transcriptomics, linking PKG, calcium, and phosphatase signaling to single-cell habituation. The Drosophila male adult nerve cord connectome analysis finds direct descending-to-motor neuron links to be rare. The dominant thread is selective, structure-aware resolution of biological organization across transcriptomic and connectomic data, although the Stentor study's genomic focus is distinct. What is new is explicit attention to the unit of analysis: high-coverage cells, contiguous genomic context, or direct circuit paths.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-07-26"
}
